<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Make up the results of a survey of a class of <m>{40}</m> learners about two subjects, Geography (<m>G</m>) and Business Studies (<m>B</m>). The results must have <m>n(G) = {15}</m>, <m>n(G \cup B) = {28}</m>, and more learners taking Business Studies than Geography. Many answers are correct.</p><p>Give the number of learners in each region.</p><p><m>G</m> only: <fillin characters="5" name="ans1"/> </p><p>both: <fillin characters="5" name="ans2"/> </p><p><m>B</m> only: <fillin characters="5" name="ans3"/> </p><p>neither: <fillin characters="5" name="ans4"/> </p><p/></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Two regions are fixed by the facts. <m>B</m> only <m>= n(G \cup B) - n(G) = {28} - {15} = {13}</m>, and neither <m>= {40} - {28} = {12}</m>.</p><p>The other two make <m>n(G) = {15}</m>: <m>G</m> only <m>+</m> both <m>= {15}</m>. For <m>n(B) &gt; n(G)</m> we need both <m>+ {13} &gt; {15}</m>, so both must be at least <m>{3}</m>. So both can be any whole number from <m>{3}</m> to <m>{15}</m>.</p><p>For example: <m>G</m> only <m>{12}</m>, both <m>{3}</m>, <m>B</m> only <m>{13}</m>, neither <m>{12}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>1</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>14</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>13</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>12</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>